      {
        "id": "rod-knock-is-the-most-serious-engine-noise-you-can-hear",
        "title": "Rod knock is the most serious engine noise you can hear",
        "description": "A deep, rhythmic knock that increases with engine RPM is rod knock. It means the bearing between the connecting rod and crankshaft has failed. Oil pressure loss is imminent. Driving for even a few minutes will destroy the crankshaft and potentially the entire engine block.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In our 24,650 quote dataset, 87% of rod knock cases resulted in engine replacement because drivers continued running the engine after symptoms appeared."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "1"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": "h3",
            "id": "",
            "text": "Detonation"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Sounds like marbles rattling in a can - occurs under load."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "£60-£400"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "2"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": "h3",
            "id": "",
            "text": "Rod knock"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Sounds like a heavy hammer hitting metal - occurs at idle, worsens with RPM."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "£800-£1,500 early"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "3"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": "h3",
            "id": "",
            "text": "Active failure"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Crankshaft damage begins. Oil pressure drops."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "£1,500-£3,500"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "4"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": "h3",
            "id": "",
            "text": "Catastrophic"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Block damaged - full engine replacement."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "£2,500-£10,000+"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "What people think vs reality"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "source": {
          "sectionId": "rod-knock-is-the-most-serious-engine-noise-you-can-hear",
          "order": 1
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "the-expensive-mistake-is-assuming-it-is-harmless",
        "title": "The expensive mistake is assuming it is harmless",
        "description": "“It's just a timing issue - I can drive it to the garage.”",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If it's rod knock, the bearing is already failing. Each revolution causes more damage. Driving even 1 mile turns an £800 repair into a £5,000+ replacement."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "“I'll add thicker oil to quieten it.”"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Thicker oil masks the noise briefly while continuing to destroy the bearing. You're silencing the symptom, not fixing the problem."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "“My car has done 100k miles; this is normal wear.”"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Rod knock is not normal at any mileage. It's a failure of the lubrication system or bearing material."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "“A mechanic can fix this with an engine flush.”"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Engine flushes do not fix mechanical bearing failure. At best, they clean the system; at worst, they accelerate failure."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Knocking types & repair costs"
          }
        ],
        "links": [],
        "source": {
          "sectionId": "the-expensive-mistake-is-assuming-it-is-harmless",
          "order": 2
        }
      },
